## Why This Is Asked
This probes understanding of iterative approaches, partition logic, and complexity trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort. 

## Key Concepts
- Iterative quicksort with explicit stack
- Hoare partition scheme and pivot strategy
- Time/space complexity comparisons with mergesort

## Code Example
```javascript
function iterativeQuicksort(a){
  const n=a.length;
  const stack=[[0,n-1]];
  const swap=(i,j)=>{let t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  while(stack.length){
    const [l,r]=stack.pop();
    if(l>=r) continue;
    let pivot=a[(l+r)>>1];
    let i=l, j=r;
    while(i<=j){
      while(a[i]<pivot) i++;
      while(a[j]>pivot) j--;
      if(i<=j){ swap(i,j); i++; j--; }
    }
    if(l<j) stack.push([l,j]);
    if(i<r) stack.push([i,r]);
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to guarantee O(n log n) worst-case?
- Compare stability with mergesort; when would you choose one over the other?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of two classic sorts, practical optimization, and memory trade-offs. A hybrid approach reveals ability to tune algorithms for real data patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting threshold and when it helps
- Stability via merge vs insertion behavior
- Space usage with a single auxiliary buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
function sortBatch(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const buf = new Array(n);
  function insertionSort(a, left, right) {
    for (let i = left + 1; i <= right; i++) {
      const key = a[i];
      let j = i - 1;
      while (j >= left && a[j] > key) {
        a[j + 1] = a[j];
        j--;
      }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function merge(left, mid, right) {
    let i = left, j = mid + 1, k = left;
    while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
      if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
      else buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    }
    while (i <= mid) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
    while (j <= right) buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    for (let t = left; t <= right; t++) arr[t] = buf[t];
  }
  function sort(l, r) {
    if (l >= r) return;
    if (r - l + 1 <= 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, l, r);
    } else {
      const m = Math.floor((l + r) / 2);
      sort(l, m);
      sort(m + 1, r);
      merge(l, m, r);
    }
  }
  sort(0, n - 1);
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests your ability to combine multiple data structures to solve a real-world analytics challenge: maintaining sliding window aggregations with fast top-K retrieval under tight latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

1. **Circular bucket array** enables O(1) bucketing and automatic expiration of old events
2. **Hashmap aggregation** provides O(1) lookup and update for per-user totals across categories
3. **Max-heap with lazy deletion** avoids the overhead of rebuilding the heap on every expiration; stale entries are filtered on-demand during `topK`
4. **Lazy expiration** ensures we never scan individual events during query time—only expired buckets trigger cleanup

## Code Example

```javascript
class WindowedTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.buckets = new Array(W).fill(null).map(() => ({ head: null, tail: null, ts: null }));
    this.totals = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
    this.head = 0;
  }

  insertEvent(ts, userId, category, value) {
    const idx = ts % this.W;
    const bucket = this.buckets[idx];
    const event = { userId, category, value, ts, next: null };
    if (!bucket.head) bucket.head = bucket.tail = event;
    else bucket.tail = bucket.tail.next = event;
    bucket.ts = ts;

    this.totals.set(userId, (this.totals.get(userId) || 0) + value);
    this.heap.push({ total: this.totals.get(userId), userId, ts });
  }

  advanceTime(now) {
    const cutoff = now - this.W;
    while (this.buckets[this.head].ts !== null && this.buckets[this.head].ts < cutoff) {
      let event = this.buckets[this.head].head;
      while (event) {
        this.totals.set(event.userId, (this.totals.get(event.userId) || 0) - event.value);
        event = event.next;
      }
      this.buckets[this.head] = { head: null, tail: null, ts: null };
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
    }
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    this.advanceTime(now);
    const result = [];
    let i = 0;
    while (result.length < K && i < this.heap.length) {
      const entry = this.heap[i++];
      if (this.totals.get(entry.userId) === entry.total) {
        result.push(entry.userId);
      }
    }
    return result;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- **Per-category top-K**: Maintain separate totals per `(userId, category)` and separate heaps for each category
- **Bounded memory**: Add a periodic rebuild phase or use LRU eviction for rarely-active user_ids
- **Distributed extension**: Partition by user_id hash across nodes, aggregate partial results

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to formulate DP with non-trivial constraints (non-adjacent picks), carefully handle feasibility, and compare memoization vs bottom-up tabulation and space optimization.

## Key Concepts
- DP state (i, t) representing position and how many selections left
- Transitions: skip i (i+1,t) and take i (a[i] + f(i+2,t-1))
- Base conditions
- Impossibility when m > ceil(n/2)
- Space optimization via rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumNonAdjacent(a, m) {
  const n = a.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+3 }, () => Array(m+1).fill(NEG));
  for (let i=0; i<=n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for (let i=n-1; i>=0; i--) {
    for (let t=1; t<=m; t++) {
      const skip = dp[i+1][t];
      const take = a[i] + dp[i+2][t-1];
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }
  return dp[0][m];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reconstruct the chosen indices?
- How to extend to at-most-m or negative values handling?
- How would you optimize to O(m) space with path reconstruction?

